faq:
  - q: How Much Does A Diagnostic Cost vs. Guessing At Repairs?
    body: >-
      Every diagnostic appointment at Duft Auto includes a written estimate before repair work begins, so the cost of fixing the actual problem stays clear before any decision gets made. Replacing a part based on a trouble code alone, without confirming the cause, risks paying for a repair that doesn't fix the symptom, then paying again for the part that actually failed. Duft Auto pairs a scan-tool code read with a physical inspection first, confirming the real cause before a single part gets ordered. This sequence keeps diagnostic cost separate from repair cost, so a customer approves a specific, confirmed repair rather than a guess.
  - q: How Do I Know My Car Needs A Diagnostic?
    items:
      - Check engine or dashboard warning light stays on
      - Rough idle, stalling, or hard starts
      - Loss of power or hesitation while driving
      - Unexplained drop in fuel economy
      - Vehicle fails to start or starts intermittently
  - q: What Does The Diagnostic Process Involve At Duft Auto?
    body: >-
      A diagnostic visit starts with a scheduled appointment — no walk-in wait, no guessing when a bay opens up. Auggie connects a scan tool to read stored trouble codes from the vehicle's computer, then follows with a physical inspection of the flagged component and related wiring. A test drive confirms intermittent faults that don't show up during a stationary scan. Findings get explained in plain language, with a written estimate covering parts and labor before any repair work starts. Most check-engine-light and drivability diagnostics complete within a single appointment; intermittent electrical faults occasionally require a follow-up test drive to confirm.
